now women earn less than men in every  Advanced economy in the world this is  despite a slew of policies aiming to  redress the balance on years of  discussion about the topic according to  the United Nations for every dollar a  man earns a woman takes home just 77  cents  a court efforts to address the issue are  well underway but at the current rate of  progress it would take  257 years to close the gender pay Gap a  recent study by the journal Nature human  behavior has taken a closer look at the  issue researchers looked at the  situation in several advanced economies  you can see a sample of them here and  the difference in Pay between men and  women in each one now the study came to  some interesting conclusions which my  colleague Arthur Sullivan has been  looking into that's got the breakdown  from him now  hi guys thanks for coming on  I'm wondering this study seemed to  really upend some of the assumptions we  have about the causes of the gender pay  Gap why is that yeah so one of the big  underlying assumptions which has been  behind a lot of the research that's been  on the gender pay gap for decades now is  that the majority of the issuer and the  peer Gap comes from things called  sorting and segregation issues in terms  of jobs so that basically means that  women end up in lower paid sectors now  obviously there are structural  um descriptive structural discrimination  within that in that a lot of those  sectors which become in quote marks  feminized are structurally undervalued  but what's fundamentally different about  this study is it shows that to a much  larger extent than previously thought  what's known as within job inequality is  actually responsible for half the pay  Gap that means men and women doing  essentially the same work or been paid  less so it's not because women are doing  different jobs that are typically paid  less it's because they're actually  simply paid less even though they are in  the same job and that accounts according  to this study for roughly half of the  pay Gap in the 15 countries that are  profiled okay so if we know that this is  happening the obvious question is what  can we do about that well one of the  things that the authors of the study say  that's so important for this study is  that there's different sets of policies  required so in cases where it's  um the segregation issue it's important  to encourage women to come into jobs  which are better paid but this other  issue this within job inequality is is  more difficult to deal with but there is  one consistent policy thread that's  required and that's pay and wage  transparency and there is some work been  done on that for example the EU has  brought in a directive which should come  into effect in the across the EU over  the next two to three years which would  dramatically change the rules on wage  impaired transparency would give  employees the right to get quite  specific details on pair structures  within their organizations also would  prevent employers from asking them about  their own salary expectations things  like that and would also give workers  the right to pursue claims if they feel  they've been unfairly discriminated and  that's one of the key areas that needs  to be addressed Germany has brought in  its own law and disregard a few years  ago there's a lot of criticism of that  law for example one of the big ones is  that you must be have at least 500  employees for a lot of its requirements  to come into effect so hopefully for for  those seeking a closing of this pay Gap  this EU directive will be a big big  changing point I want to talk a little  bit more about Germany specifically  because it has one of the highest gender  pay gaps in the EU and that's despite  some pretty Progressive policies you  mentioned ever to change there but  looking specifically at parental leave  we have this very generous policy where  mothers and fathers can divide their  time equally but that doesn't seem to  happen why is there a motherhood penalty  here and no fatherhood penalty yeah it's  one of the really interesting things  about this study and the authors  themselves have said that Germany is a  bit of a puzzle to them because Germany  does indeed have those Progressive  policies policies one of the best  paternity benefit systems in the world  and yet there is this consistent  motherhood penalty in Germany and also  the a pay gap which does lag behind a  lot of the equivalent sort of peers in  northern Europe especially now it's hard  to say exactly why that is but one of  the things is that women because of  pre-existing inequalities tend to be in  the Lesser paid roles and so it's a  self-reinforcing issue that women will  tend then to take a little bit more  leave obviously has more there's also  because of the fact of pregnancy they  will tend to take a little bit longer  there may be health issues during  pregnancy and things like that and they  may miss out on opportunities during  that period it also tends to occur that  German women often will get pregnant in  their 30s and that's obviously a  critical time in career development so  there are these issues that need to be  addressed and again policies have to  come in to further level the playing  field for women to take into account  these issues another issue very briefly  within Germany is that it's a lot of its  economic Edge comes in industrial  sectors and there still is not of  recommend representational women in  those areas okay Arthur Sullivan thank  you so much
 

